tgloggerd web application
=========================

The React application behind the tgloggerd web UI. Pages are rendered
to HTML on the server and then taken over by the browser; the design
is in ../../docs/design/web-ssr.md (server rendering and the render
protocol) and ../../docs/design/web-live-client.md (live updates). The
visual design comes from ../mockup.

  - The Go service (webapi/) authenticates, applies access rules, loads
    each page's data and serves the API under /api/.
  - The render service in server/ is a small Node process that turns a
    page's route and data into HTML for the Go service, over a Unix
    socket. It has no database access, cookies or secrets.
  - The browser bundle (src/entry-client.tsx) hydrates that HTML and
    navigates without reloads, fetching page data from /api/page/.

Everything runs through Docker; nothing but Docker is needed on the
host:

    ./dev npm ci
    ./dev npm run typecheck
    ./dev npm test
    ./dev npm run build
    ./dev npm run check:api

`npm run build` (scripts/build.ts) writes the client bundle to
dist/client (content-hashed files and the manifest the Go service
reads) and the render entry to dist/server, both with one build id,
and records that id last in dist/client/build.json, where the Go
service reads it. The id is a hash of the inputs (package.json, the
lock file, tsconfig.json, vite.config.ts, and src/ without its tests),
so the same tree gives the same id; TGD_BUILD (passed through by
./dev) sets another.

The API's types are generated from the Go service's OpenAPI
description, ../../webapi/openapi.yaml, into src/api/generated/ by
`npm run gen:api`, and committed. After changing the description, run
it and commit the result with the change; `npm run check:api` fails
while the committed file differs from what the description gives.

Deployment
----------

docker-compose.yml runs the render service as the "render" service
(container tgloggerd-render): Node pinned to the image ./dev uses, the
built web/app mounted read-only, no network, no .env, a read-only file
system, no capabilities, restarted when it exits. It creates its
socket in the render-socket volume, a tmpfs owned by TG_UID:TG_GID
that only it and the webapi service mount, at the path both use by
default (RENDER_SOCKET, /run/tgloggerd-render/render.sock). It logs
one JSON line per render to stdout (request id, route, status, bytes,
ms); the compose health check runs server/health.ts (GET /health).

The build is a separate one-off service, render-build, the only step
that needs the network (the npm registry, over Docker's default
bridge): npm ci from the lock file, then npm run build, as TG_UID.
It writes dist/server for the render service and dist/client (with
build.json) for the webapi service, which serves it under
/app/assets/ from WEBAPI_APP_DIST (default web/app/dist/client), so
both always come from the same build with the same id.

First deployment, from the repository root:

    # .env: WEBAPI_APP_BUILD empty or absent (the id comes from
    # build.json); RENDER_SOCKET, WEBAPI_APP_DIST at their defaults.
    docker compose run --rm render-build
    docker compose up -d render
    docker compose ps render            # wait for "healthy"
    docker compose up -d webapi         # recreated: mounts the socket
                                        # volume, reads the new bundle

After a change under web/app:

    docker compose run --rm render-build
    docker compose restart render webapi

The webapi service reads the manifest and build id at start only, so
it is restarted after every build, after the render service. Between
the build and that restart the render service may answer 409 for the
old id, and pages fall back to browser rendering meanwhile. A build
adds its assets beside those of earlier builds instead of replacing
them, so pages linked before the restart, and pages still open in a
browser, keep working; assets that none of the last three builds uses
are removed (dist/client/.builds.json records which build used which).

RENDER_WORKERS in the environment of `docker compose` (or .env, read
for interpolation only) sets the number of render processes,
default 2.

scripts/test-render-service.sh tests all of this on a copy of the
tree, in a compose project of its own.
